    <semantic:dataStore id="_deee9f47-28cb-4756-9d63-bdce943ea96b" name="Rewards System DB" triso:readOnly="false" itemSubjectRef="_triso-default-bpmnItemDefinition-Any_id" triso:displayName="Rewards System DB" isUnlimited="false"/>
    <semantic:process id="_84483335-e96e-46cb-bf50-91dd22c41c4c" name="bad" triso:readOnly="false" triso:collectEnds="true" isClosed="false">
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_80f059cb-787f-4abc-b83d-9c45b7d5d09f">
            <semantic:text>two examples, bad and good in terms of crossing flows, message flows, and associations</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_861f5ad1-4518-43be-bae7-0af14d3688f4">
            <semantic:text>Define Avoid crossing flows in BPMN terms.
Identify when to apply Avoid crossing flows.
Model a 4-6 step example using Avoid crossing flows correctly.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_57a83a31-ba7a-467f-8bd3-a788f48670c5">
            <semantic:text>Today's concept: Avoid crossing flows. In a travel rewards transfer scenario, ask: What starts the flow, where can it split, how does it rejoin, and what ends it? Name tasks verb-object and avoid crossing lines. If a decision is needed, pick the gateway that matches the semantics. If data crosses pools, use a message flow, not a sequence flow. Keep one clear idea per diagram. Quick mental model: make it obvious, and test it with token animation if your tool supports it. Try modeling it in under 60 seconds.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
    </semantic:process>
